WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.820
Wow, Oscar… you sure have a selective memory.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:07.440
Per tradition, Sunday night's Academy Awards
telecast featured an "In Memoriam" segment

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.230
that honored notable personalities we've lost
over the last year and, also per tradition,

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:15.500
a number of beloved entertainment giants were
inexplicably omitted.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.440
The Twitterverse quickly took notice, and,
as you might imagine, folks had some pretty

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:20.880
harsh words for the Academy.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.880
Among this year's many snubs, veteran character
actor Dick Miller is one of the most notable

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.550
celebrities who didn't make the cut.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.320
"I expect to see you in the funnies any day
now… with Smiling Jack and Little Abner."

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.440
He's perhaps best known for his role in the
1984 Joe Dante classic Gremlins.

00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:40.690
Miller's prolific career spanned six decades,
with his filmography stretching from the '50s

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:41.900
until just last year.

00:00:42.069 --> 00:00:43.420
The pop culture website A.V.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.190
Club also pointed out the snub, noting that
Miller's

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.220
"... ubiquity as a character actor sadly didn't
translate to the Oscars."

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.800
The name Gary Kurtz probably rings a bell.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.940
Although his body of work wasn't exactly a
mile long, his legacy lies in a little film

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.120
he took a chance on back in the 1970s.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.260
"Oh, you said it, Chewy."

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.220
In case you somehow haven't guessed, that
film is Star Wars, which Kurtz co-produced

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.070
along with The Empire Strikes Back — not
to mention Jim Henson's 1982 fantasy classic

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:14.220
The Dark Crystal.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.900
"Oh no, right in the mud."

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.380
If that's not an impressive resume, we really
don't know what is.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:31.840
How could the Academy fail to mention Carol
Channing, who passed away on January 15, 2019?

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.750
In 1968, the legendary entertainer was nominated
for a Best Supporting Actress Oscar for her

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.800
performance in Thoroughly Modern Millie.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.840
"Raspberries!"

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.659
She's even presented at the Oscars before.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:51.420
"And what a thrill it is to fondle an Oscar
even in transit."

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.780
Still, Channing got no love from the Academy
at this year's ceremony.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.640
That obviously didn't sit well with one Twitter
user, who simply stated that Channing was

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.860
an Oscar nominee over and over until he ran
out of characters.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:03.860
Can you blame him?

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.820
The Academy also failed to mention Verne Troyer,
who shot to worldwide fame as Mini-Me in the

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:09.940
Austin Powers films.

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:14.540
Where was Al Matthews, who appeared in James
Cameron's 1986 masterpiece Aliens, one of

00:02:14.549 --> 00:02:16.620
the greatest science fiction films of all
time?

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.819
What about veteran character actor R. Lee
Ermey, who's scene-stealing performance in

00:02:20.819 --> 00:02:24.249
Full Metal Jacket made him one of the most
memorable film characters ever?

00:02:24.249 --> 00:02:26.900
"Why, you little maggot, you make me want
to vomit!"

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.099
And then there's John Mahoney, best known
for his role as Martin Crane in the long-running

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.560
TV series Frasier.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.980
That's a lot of amazing talent... but we saved
the biggest omission for last.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:38.960
"You better think about what you saying.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.320
You better think about the consequences of
your actions."

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:42.720
That's right.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.080
Aretha Franklin, the Queen of Soul, wasn't
included in the memorial... despite being

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.180
one of the greatest vocalists the world has
ever seen… or heard rather.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.819
She had a number of film and television credits
on her resume, particularly a standout appearance

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.120
in the 1980 classic The Blues Brothers.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.340
"Now you're not going back on the road no'
mo'... and you ain't playing no two-bit sleazy

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:01.840
dive."

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.680
Each year, the "In Memoriam" segment serves
as a way for good ol' Oscar to shoot himself

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:07.680
in the foot.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.680
In that regard, the 2019 edition did not disappoint.

